begin model
# =====================================================================
# FluBNF SIHRS-NATG: production `min` PLUS one exogenous term, zero new
# fitted parameters. This file is a copy of templates/SIHRS_pop_min.bngl
# with the national-growth factor added to beta(t) and NOTHING else
# changed. SIHRS_pop_min.bngl stays the untouched production control.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# BUILD 1: EXOGENOUS NATIONAL GROWTH  (handoff 2026-08-21, section 3)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   beta_s(t)  *=  exp( iota * ( g_nat^{-s}(t) - g_s^obs(t) ) )
#
# g_nat^{-s} is the population-weighted LEAVE-ONE-OUT log-growth of
# vintage-true weekly admissions across all OTHER FluSight jurisdictions,
# as of the same as-of date. g_s^obs is this state's own most recent
# vintage-true log-growth. Both are built OUTSIDE the BNGL, at
# materialize time, by flubnf/natgrowth.py, from
# app.core.data.vintage_path(asof) -- never from the latest file.
#
# WHY, MEASURED. Leave-one-out national growth at week t predicts a
# state's own growth at t+1 AFTER the state's AR(1) and a Fourier
# seasonal -- i.e. after exactly the information eps1/phi1 already give
# this model. Partial correlation +0.469. LOSO delta-R^2 +0.081 /
# +0.032 / +0.118 and turn-week RMSE reductions +8.9% / +2.4% / +14.7%
# over 2023-24 / 2024-25 / 2025-26. This is THIS year's realised wave,
# not the calendar: nothing in the production system currently sees
# "the Midwest peaked last week". Detail:
# research/spatial-nowcast-probe/FINDINGS.md section 1.
#
# ON GROWTH, NEVER ON LEVEL. A level-form importation term
# sum_s' w_ss' * A_s'/N_s' restates prevalence, which the filter already
# has (the occupancy-ratio trap). The DIFFERENCE form is neutral by
# construction: a state growing at the national rate gets a multiplier
# of exactly 1, so the term can only speak when the state and the
# country disagree.
#
# NOT A METAPOPULATION. The ODEs are not coupled, no species are added,
# no movement rates exist. Per-state filters, 10k particles, 5 fitted
# parameters and per-state parallelism are all unchanged. A coupled
# 52-region model was measured and rejected (FINDINGS.md section 1).
#
# IOTA IS FROZEN A PRIORI. IT IS NOT A FITTED PARAMETER.
#   OLS of next-week own log-growth on leave-one-out national growth,
#   given own lag-1 growth and a first Fourier harmonic on week-of-
#   season (probe.py::spatial()'s design matrix), fitted separately on
#   the two seasons the handoff names:
#       2023-24  n=1393  b[g_nat] = 0.7574
#       2024-25  n=1418  b[g_nat] = 0.4504
#       mean                        0.6039
#       x0.5 shrink toward zero  -> 0.3020   <- iota
#   Cross-check: pooling those two seasons gives b = 0.5528 -> 0.276,
#   the same number within the season-to-season spread. 2025-26 is NOT
#   in the average (its own coefficient is 0.8040, so the exclusion made
#   iota smaller, not larger). Reproduce:
#     ./.venv/bin/python research/spatial-nowcast-probe/iota_freeze.py
#   The constant lives in flubnf/natgrowth.py::IOTA_FROZEN. It must
#   NEVER appear as a *__FREE var line, and it must never be retuned
#   after seeing scores (handoff Law 1).
#
#   HONEST NOTE ON EFFECTIVE STRENGTH. iota multiplies beta, not the
#   growth rate. exp(iota*gap) shifts weekly log-growth by roughly
#   gamma*Reff*iota*gap, and gamma is 2.19/week, so the implemented
#   response is ~0.66 per unit gap: the x0.5 shrink is approximately
#   cancelled by that amplification and the term lands near 1.0x the
#   regression coefficient rather than 0.5x. Recorded here so nobody
#   reads 0.302 as "half strength" when reporting the arm.
#
# FORECAST WEEKS, THE PRE-REGISTERED RULE. g_nat is unknown at h=1..4.
# THE LAST OBSERVED (g_nat - g_s) GAP IS HELD CONSTANT ACROSS THE 1 TO 4
# WEEK HORIZON. No extrapolation, no decay toward zero, no forecast of
# the national series. The rule is structural, not a code path: the
# final branch of the piecewise expression below carries no upper guard,
# so every t at or beyond the last observed week takes that value.
#
# ALIGNMENT AND CAUSALITY. The filter integrates one segment per
# observation, [w-1, w]. The piecewise value on [w-1, w) is the gap
# realised up to and including week w-1, i.e. information that existed
# before that week began. No look-ahead.
#
# NUMERICAL GUARD. The gap is clipped to +-1.0 log-growth units before
# iota is applied (flubnf/natgrowth.py::GAP_CLIP), so the beta
# multiplier stays inside [0.74, 1.35]. beta enters as beta0*exp(...)
# and the eps1 bounds are already documented below as stiffness-
# critical; one state reporting 2 admissions after 40 must not hand
# exp() an argument no solver should see. Clipped weeks are counted and
# reported per cell, never silently absorbed.
#
# Tokens this variant adds, on top of the ones `min` already needs:
# IOTA, GAPEXPR, GAPNOTE -- written without their braces here so this
# comment survives materialization instead of being substituted into an
# unreadable line. flubnf/natgrowth.py::natg_tokens supplies all three.
# =====================================================================
#
# =====================================================================
# FluBNF SIHRS — PARSIMONIOUS (5 fitted parameters, not 8)
#
# WHY FEWER PARAMETERS, MEASURED
# ------------------------------
# The entire gap between SIHRS and a well-calibrated reference is SPREAD, not
# the central estimate. Swapping SIHRS's median for the analogue's gains 0.003
# relWIS; swapping the SPREAD gains 0.070. So the median is fine and the
# predictive distribution is too wide.
#
# That width comes from fitting 8 parameters to ~26 weekly points. Each removed
# dimension removes a source of posterior spread, which is the actual defect.
# Removed here, each for a measured reason:
#
#   eps2, phi2  semi-annual harmonic. An earlier ablation found it makes beta(t)
#               two-humped but does NOT produce two epidemic peaks -- susceptible
#               depletion suppresses the second hump. R-hat 2.38 / 26.8.
#   impr        external reseeding, PINNED IN 75% OF FITS, i.e. not identified by
#               the data. It exists to keep I off the numerical floor across
#               MULTI-season runs (230 weeks); over a single 48-week season it is
#               inert -- verified: peak 142.6 with impr=1e-7 and with impr=0,
#               and I never approaches the denormal range.
#
# KEEP `impr` FOR MULTI-SEASON WORK. Dropping it there reintroduces the CVODE
# stiffness failure that once broke 100% of 230-week fits. This template is for
# single-season production fits only; use SIHRS_pop.bngl for multi-season.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# POPULATION-PARAMETERIZED (no magnitude anchor)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same science as models/SIHRS.bngl (real H compartment fed by the I->H
# flux, waning R->S, continuous 2-harmonic Fourier beta(t)), but the
# compartments are ABSOLUTE PEOPLE and the state population N enters as
# known data instead of via a fitted/derived `scaled` anchor.
#
# Why: with S(0)=1 normalization, matching an observed peak P required
#   mult * scaled_factor = 1/(5.5*f) ~= 30  (f = max_t[rho*gamma*I]) --
# INDEPENDENT of P, so it carried no per-state information and sat ~6x
# above the mult prior ceiling. `mult` therefore pinned in 43/52 states
# and forecasts came out ~30x low. With N in the model the observation
# scale is a dimensionless fraction and no anchor is needed.
#
# Infection is FREQUENCY-DEPENDENT (rate constant beta()/N, flux
# beta*S*I/N). That keeps beta on exactly the same scale as the
# normalized model -- substituting s=S/N, i=I/N recovers
# di/dt = beta*s*i - gamma*i identically -- so R0/gamma/eps/phi priors
# transfer unchanged and no re-derivation is needed.
#
# Tokens substituted per state at materialization:
#   {{POP}} {{S0FRAC}} {{I0FRAC}} {{GAMMA}} {{RHO}} {{GAMMAH}} {{OMEGA}}
# Fitted (5): Reff, eps1, phi1, mult, r.  Everything else is sourced --
# see flubnf/sihrs_priors.py for the DOI/derivation of each.
#
# Fit the REPORTED admission flux (rhomult*gamma*I), never the census H(t).
# =====================================================================
begin parameters

### -- transmission: scale-free reproduction number + seasonal forcing -- ###
Reff    Reff__FREE     # EFFECTIVE reproduction number at season start.
                       # Fit this, NOT R0: admissions identify Reff (via the growth
                       # rate), and with S(0)=N*s0 the classic R0 = Reff/s0 is only
                       # recoverable post hoc. Fitting R0 with s0<1 silently
                       # requires R0>1/s0 just to sustain an epidemic.
eps1    eps1__FREE     # annual-harmonic amplitude
phi1    phi1__FREE     # annual-harmonic phase (week of peak transmissibility)

### -- progression / removal -- ###
gamma   {{GAMMA}}      # FIXED: 7/3.2 d generation time, Chan 2024 doi:10.1101/2024.08.17.24312064
rho     {{RHO}}        # FIXED: true IHR = biological branching fraction I->H.
                       # Used ONLY in the reaction rules (people's flow), never in
                       # the observable.
mult    mult__FREE     # ASCERTAINMENT: reported / modelled admissions. Fitted, with a
                       # narrow prior [0.002,0.10] measured across all 52 states, so it
                       # is still universal. Only rho*mult is identified, but the
                       # product CANNOT be pinned a priori: it depends on the attack
                       # rate, which is itself a consequence of the fitted Reff.
                       # Pinning it froze the magnitude 2.45x wrong and drove r to its
                       # floor. So fix rho (biology) and fit mult (observation).
gammaH  {{GAMMAH}}     # FIXED: discharge rate. Does NOT enter the admissions fit
                       # target at all (H census only), so it is unidentifiable here.
omega   {{OMEGA}}      # FIXED: waning R->S. Weakly identified from <3 seasons.

### -- external force of infection (reseeding) -- ###
# Per-susceptible weekly hazard of infection from OUTSIDE this population.
# Required, not cosmetic: waning refills the FUEL (S) but supplies no SPARK (I),
# and infection is S+I->I+I i.e. proportional to I. Between seasons I decays for
# ~30 weeks and, in absolute counts, reaches ~1e-150 people -- biologically
# meaningless and numerically denormal, which made CVODE fail on 100% of
# 230-week multi-season fits. Real flu is REIMPORTED each autumn from other
# populations; a closed single-population ODE cannot represent that.
# Written as S()->I() (not 0->I()) so total population is conserved.
# FITTED: no clean literature value exists for a per-state weekly reseeding rate.
# It also separates from i0 multi-season, because i0 affects only the FIRST
# season while importation drives EVERY season's onset.

### -- observation noise (the only reporting parameter still fitted) -- ###
r       r__FREE        # negative-binomial dispersion (AMCMC)

### -- exogenous national growth: FIXED A PRIORI, never fitted -- ###
iota    {{IOTA}}       # FROZEN. Derivation in this file's header and in
                       # flubnf/natgrowth.py::IOTA_FROZEN. Deliberately a plain
                       # parameter, NOT a *__FREE var: fitting it would be a
                       # coupling strength fitted on past-season data, which
                       # Law 1 forbids.

### -- derived -- ###
beta0   Reff*gamma/s0
pi      3.141592653589793  # avoid BNGL _pi(): bngsim codegen emits M_PI(), which will not compile

### -- FIXED per-state structural constants (known data, never fitted) -- ###
N       {{POP}}        # state population (absolute people)
s0      {{S0FRAC}}     # initial susceptible fraction; FIXED -- R0*s0 is product-identified
i0      {{I0FRAC}}     # initial infected fraction

end parameters

begin molecule types
S()         # susceptible people
I()         # infectious people
H()         # hospitalized census / occupancy
R()         # recovered / immune people
Hadm()      # cumulative admissions accumulator (never decays)
counter()   # clock: observable t = weeks
end molecule types

begin seed species
# Absolute counts. R(0) carries the non-susceptible, non-infected remainder,
# so S+I+H+R = N exactly and pre-existing immunity is representable (s0 < 1),
# which the S(0)=1 normalization structurally cannot express.
S() N*s0
I() N*i0
H() 0
R() N*(1-s0-i0)
Hadm() 0
counter() 0
end seed species

begin observables
Molecules S     S()
Molecules I     I()
Molecules H     H()        # census -- do NOT fit to an admissions target
Molecules R     R()
Molecules H_Cum Hadm()     # cumulative admissions; neg_bin_dynamic differences '_Cum' -> weekly
Molecules t     counter()
end observables

begin functions

### -- exogenous national-growth gap, piecewise-constant in t -- ###
# {{GAPNOTE}}
# Generated at materialize time by flubnf/natgrowth.py::bngl_gap_expression.
# Value on [w, w+1) is g_nat^{-s}(w) - g_s^obs(w), clipped to +-1.0; runs of
# equal values are merged. The final branch has NO upper guard, which IS the
# pre-registered forecast rule: t at or beyond the last observed week holds the
# last observed gap. A week with no defined growth (missing data, or either
# endpoint under the 5-admission floor) contributes exactly 0.0, i.e. the
# production model.
natgap() = {{GAPEXPR}}

### -- continuous seasonal transmission rate; exp() keeps beta > 0 -- ###
# The ONLY change from SIHRS_pop_min.bngl: the exp(iota*natgap()) factor.
# iota = 0 reduces this file to the production template exactly.
beta() = beta0*exp( eps1*cos(2*pi*(t-phi1)/52) )*exp( iota*natgap() )

### -- weekly REPORTED admissions = ascertained flux into H -- ###
# rho*mult carries biological IHR x reporting ascertainment. `rho` also
# appears in the reaction rules (it is a real branching fraction); `mult`
# does NOT -- ascertainment must never alter how people move between
# compartments, only what we observe of them.
H_weekly() = rho*mult*gamma*I

end functions

begin reaction rules
# clock
0 -> counter() 1
# infection, FREQUENCY-DEPENDENT: rate constant beta()/N -> flux beta*S*I/N
S() + I() -> I() + I()   beta()/N
# hospitalization branch: fraction rho of the gamma removal
I() -> H()   rho*gamma
# direct-recovery branch
I() -> R()   (1-rho)*gamma
# discharge
H() -> R()   gammaH
# waning immunity (the second S in SIHRS)
R() -> S()   omega
# cumulative-admissions accumulator (counts I->H without consuming I)
I() -> I() + Hadm()   rho*gamma
end reaction rules

end model

begin actions
generate_network({overwrite=>1})
simulate({suffix=>"SIHRS_flu",method=>"ode",t_start=>0,t_end=>48,n_steps=>48,print_functions=>1})
end actions
